          "explanation": "The subject of the verb is the plural noun 'contributions,' which requires the plural verb 'are.' The intervening prepositional phrase 'to laser cataract surgery' contains the singular noun 'surgery,' but a prepositional phrase placed between a subject and its verb does not change the number of the subject. A plural subject takes a plural verb regardless of intervening singular modifiers, so 'contributions . . . are frequently highlighted' is the correct construction.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (A) 'is' is a singular verb that incorrectly agrees with the closest noun ('surgery') rather than with the actual plural subject ('contributions'). This is the classic 'proximity agreement' error.\n- (B) 'was' shifts the tense to simple past and is also singular; it commits both a number error and a tense error, since the surrounding sentence describes an ongoing pattern in the present.\n- (C) 'has been' is singular and uses present perfect aspect; like (A), it agrees with the nearer singular noun rather than the plural subject 'contributions.'"
