          "passage": "Before 2012, editing a specific gene in a living organism was a slow and expensive undertaking: each new target sequence required scientists to design and synthesize a custom protein from scratch, a process that could take months of laboratory work, and even then the resulting edits were often imprecise or unreliable. ______ molecular biologist Jennifer Doudna and her collaborator Emmanuelle Charpentier reported that the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 system could be reprogrammed with a short guide RNA to cut virtually any DNA sequence in any organism, giving researchers a fast, accurate, and easily redirected gene-editing tool.",
