          "explanation": "The first sentence establishes the prior state of gene editing: it was slow, expensive, and often imprecise. The second sentence then announces a 2012 breakthrough by Doudna and Charpentier that overturns those limitations, describing a tool that is fast, accurate, and easily redirected. The logical relation between the two sentences is reversal of expectation — readers expect the difficulty to continue, but the second sentence introduces a finding that supersedes it. 'However,' is the standard transition for that simple reversal and is the correct choice (A).\n\nThe key contrast move here is one-directional: prior limits were overcome by a new finding. 'However,' captures that 'but then this happened' shift cleanly, without implying a parallel side-by-side comparison of two named methods.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (B) 'By contrast,' also signals contrast, which makes it the strongest distractor. But 'By contrast,' is best when the two sentences set two named, parallel subjects against each other in a direct A-vs-B comparison (e.g., 'Method A is slow. By contrast, Method B is fast.'). Sentence 1 here describes the general state of the field rather than a single named method serving as one side of a parallel contrast, so 'However,' fits the reversal more naturally.\n- (D) 'In addition,' signals that more information is being added on the same line of thought. Topically, both sentences are about gene editing, so the choice can feel like a smooth continuation — but the second sentence does not add another difficulty to the first; it overturns the difficulties, which is contrast, not addition.\n- (C) 'Therefore,' signals a causal consequence: because the prior process was slow and imprecise, something logically follows. The second sentence is not a consequence of the earlier limits — it is a finding that overcomes them. 'Therefore,' would actually point in the opposite direction of the real relation."
