          "type": "multiple-choice",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "band": 4,
          "domain": "craft-and-structure",
          "skill": "words-in-context",
          "passage": "Evolutionary biologist Lynn Margulis of the University of Massachusetts Amherst spent decades championing the endosymbiotic theory, the proposition that mitochondria and chloroplasts originated as free-living bacteria engulfed by ancestral host cells. Her 1967 paper \"On the Origin of Mitosing Cells\" was rejected by more than a dozen journals before publication, in part because mainstream biologists found its central thesis difficult to credit: that the entire eukaryotic lineage was ______ a series of ancient mergers between unrelated microbes.",
          "question": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "id": "A",
              "text": "at variance with"
            },
            {
              "id": "B",
              "text": "predicated on"
            },
            {
              "id": "C",
              "text": "borne out by"
            },
            {
              "id": "D",
              "text": "commensurate with"
            }
          ],
          "correctAnswer": "B",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is B, \"predicated on.\" The passage frames Margulis's central thesis as the claim that eukaryotic cells came into being through a sequence of mergers in which one microbe engulfed another, and that this fact is the foundation of the entire eukaryotic lineage. The blank therefore needs a phrase that names a foundational, originating dependence — the lineage rests on the mergers as on a logical premise. \"Predicated on\" fits exactly: to predicate one thing on another is to establish it on that thing as its underlying basis, which is precisely the structural relation Margulis's theory posits between eukaryotes and ancient symbiotic events.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice C: surface match — \"borne out by\" collocates strongly with theories and evidence in scientific prose (\"the hypothesis was borne out by the data\"), so the phrase feels at home in a passage about a contested biological claim. But \"borne out by\" means confirmed or validated by, and what the thesis asserts is not that the lineage is confirmed by the mergers but that the lineage arose from them. The phrase carries the right scientific register while attaching it to the wrong logical relation, drawing students who match on tone rather than on the structural meaning the sentence requires.\n- Choice D: scope shift — \"commensurate with\" has the right general flavor (a fitting correspondence between two things) but the wrong specificity. To be commensurate with something is to be proportional or equivalent to it in scale or degree; the phrase names a relation of matching magnitude, not a relation of foundational origin. Margulis's claim is not that the eukaryotic lineage is proportionate to the mergers but that the mergers are what the lineage rests on, so the phrase shifts the relation from grounding to mere correspondence.\n- Choice A: inverse — \"at variance with\" is the near-opposite of what the sentence requires. To be at variance with something is to be in disagreement or conflict with it; the entire thesis Margulis was defending holds that eukaryotes came into being through these mergers, not in opposition to them. Choosing this phrase would make the lineage and the mergers stand against each other, reversing the foundational relationship the passage spells out."
