          "band": 3,
          "domain": "craft-and-structure",
          "skill": "text-structure-and-purpose",
          "passage": "The following text is adapted from Frederick Douglass's 1848 \"Letter to Thomas Auld,\" an open letter addressed to the man who had once held Douglass in bondage in Maryland. Douglass had escaped in 1838 and was by this time a public abolitionist in the North.\n\nYou will perhaps remember the morning, now eight years gone, on which I left your house for the last time and turned my face toward the North; I have not forgotten it, nor any of the calculations that preceded it. I write to you, however, not to recount my journey, but to set before you a question I have so far been unable to lay aside. Suppose, sir, that your own little Amanda, who was a child when I knew her, were torn from your roof tomorrow by men acting under color of law, carried to a distant state, and sold to a master whose name and disposition you would never learn. Consider, in such a case, what comfort the bare legality of the thing would furnish you, and what you would be moved to feel toward those who had arranged it. I ask you to feel it once, as a father, and then to remember that what you would not endure for an hour has been the daily portion of fathers I have known.",
