              "text": "The plant geneticist Pamela Ronald shares Shiva's concern that smallholder farmers in the Global South often bear the costs of agricultural change without sharing in its benefits. She is unwilling, however, to organize the question around a single contrast between industrial monoculture and traditional polyculture. Consider, she suggests, the case of submergence-tolerant rice. The relevant trait was identified through marker-assisted breeding from a long-standing Indian landrace and introduced into cultivars that resource-poor farmers in Bangladesh and eastern India were already growing. The trait raised yields under flooding without requiring any of the input intensification that Shiva associates with industrial agriculture; it left existing rotations, intercropping, and seed-saving practices intact. Examples of this kind, in Ronald's view, suggest that the meaningful unit of evaluation is not the broad category \"biotechnology\" set against the broad category \"agroecology,\" but the particular trait, the breeding pathway, the seed-distribution arrangement, and the agronomic context in which a variety is deployed. The question worth asking, she argues, is whether a given intervention — judged trait by trait — strengthens smallholder resilience or undermines it."
