          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "In her 2009 book Satellite Remote Sensing for Archaeology, archaeologist Sarah Parcak of the University of Alabama at Birmingham describes how multispectral satellite imagery can locate ancient structures that lie buried beneath the surface. Walls, roads, and foundations hidden underground subtly alter the soil and plants directly above them: moisture levels shift where buried stone interrupts drainage, and the vegetation rooted in that soil grows at slightly different rates or shades of green. Sensors that record many narrow bands of reflected light, including bands beyond the visible spectrum, can register these faint contrasts even when nothing on the ground looks unusual to an observer. Parcak has used the approach to map unexcavated features at Egyptian sites and to identify pits left by looters in conflict zones. In her account, satellite analysis offers archaeologists a way to detect buried remains across large landscapes without first turning a single shovel of earth.",
