          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "In the early 1970s, pharmacologist Tu Youyou led a team at the China Academy of Traditional Chinese Medicine searching for a new treatment for malaria as part of Project 523, a Chinese government research effort. Standard chemical screens of hundreds of plant extracts had produced disappointing results: candidate compounds either lost potency during processing or showed too little activity in animal tests. Reviewing centuries-old Chinese medical texts, Tu noticed that one source described preparing the herb Artemisia annua, known as qinghao, by soaking it in cool water rather than boiling it. That detail led her to try a low-temperature ether extraction, a method that preserved the herb's active compound where heat-based methods had destroyed it. The substance she isolated, qinghaosu—now called artemisinin—proved highly effective against malaria parasites and became the basis of frontline antimalarial therapy, work for which Tu was awarded a share of the 2015 Nobel Prize.",
