          "correctAnswer": "D",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The passage is built on a headline-then-refinement structure characteristic of hard-band central-ideas items. The opening sentences set up an apparent claim — that Hahn and Strassmann's neutron bombardment of uranium produced an anomalous chemical result, the appearance of barium — but the passage's main argument is the theoretical refinement that follows: Meitner and Frisch reinterpreted that anomaly by picturing the uranium nucleus as a liquid drop that could elongate and split into two lighter nuclei, releasing a large amount of energy in a new type of reaction they named fission. Choice D captures both the interpretive move (the liquid drop reasoning, the splitting) and its conceptual upshot (the naming of fission) at the right level of generalization.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice C (surface match — the headline claim): This restates the apparent claim that opens the passage — that Hahn and Strassmann's neutron bombardment yielded surprising chemistry — without naming the interpretive refinement that gives the passage its point. The chemical anomaly is the prompt for Meitner's argument, not the passage's main idea, so taking the headline at face value mistakes setup for thesis.\n- Choice B (inverse): This negates the passage's central refinement. The text says Meitner and Frisch's liquid drop reasoning did account for the anomaly and yielded a new concept of nuclear reaction; choice B claims the model could not account for the findings and left them unexplained. Switching the explanatory verdict from successful to unsuccessful flips the main idea.\n- Choice A (scope shift): The passage does mention that Meitner had fled to Sweden, but this biographical aside is supporting context, not the main argument. Treating the circumstances of Meitner's exile as the thesis substitutes a sub-detail for the conceptual claim about fission that the passage actually develops."
