          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises: (1) a spatiometric study of stone-built funerary monuments in southeastern Mauritania reveals an organized Late Neolithic pastoral society rooted in the Sahara, and (2) the archaeological record at sites linked to the early Ghana Empire shows a long, locally grounded sequence of agricultural and craft intensification before statehood. Because both the Saharan pastoral landscape and the economic base of Ghana developed in place, the minimum inference the passage licenses is that complex societies in this part of West Africa drew significantly on indigenous developments rather than primarily on external introductions. Choice C captures exactly this measured claim, using the hedged phrasing 'drew significantly' and 'rather than depending primarily,' which matches the two specific lines of evidence without overreaching.\n\nThe stem requires the conclusion that the two premises jointly force, not a stronger claim that exceeds the evidence and not a weaker claim that ignores half of it. Choice C is the only option that integrates both findings while staying within their scope.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (Scope shift): Pushes a measured claim about indigenous developments into an absolute denial that any external contact 'ever played any role' in shaping early West African societies. The premises support a comparative conclusion about the primary basis of these societies, not a categorical rejection of all outside influence.\n- Choice D (Surface match): Sounds consistent with the passage's positive framing of Holl's spatiometric work, but the premises license a substantive conclusion about the origins of these societies, not a methodological claim that his techniques can settle 'any remaining disputes.' Nothing in the passage compares his methods to alternatives or evaluates their reach beyond the cases described.\n- Choice B (Inverse): Directly contradicts premise (2). The passage states that the Ghana Empire's foundations were locally grounded in agricultural and craft intensification preceding statehood, so the empire did not arise 'chiefly through political imposition by polities outside the region.'"
