          "difficulty": "hard",
          "band": 4,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "Astrophysicist Andrea Ghez, working at the University of California, Los Angeles, has spent decades using adaptive optics on the twin Keck telescopes to follow individual stars threading the crowded region around Sagittarius A*, at the center of the Milky Way. Over the course of repeated observations, the orbit of one bright star known as S0-2 has been traced through a complete loop around that point. The shape of S0-2's path, together with the timing of its sweep around the focus, can be reproduced by a model in which a single compact mass of several million solar masses governs the motion. Because the orbit's geometry and period align with that simple gravitational picture, Ghez's measurements support the conclusion that the central object ______",
