          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises: (1) Ghez has tracked the orbit of S0-2 around Sagittarius A* through a complete loop using adaptive optics on the Keck telescopes, and (2) the geometry and period of that orbit can be reproduced by a model in which a single compact mass of several million solar masses sits at the focus. The minimum inference these premises jointly force is that the central object behaves the way a supermassive black hole would behave gravitationally — its kinematic signature matches that prediction. Choice B states exactly that hedged claim, using 'exhibits the kinematic signature one would expect' rather than asserting that the object has been proven to be a black hole. The premises license consistency with a supermassive black hole model, which is what A captures.\n\nAt this difficulty, the test is sufficient-vs-necessary: the orbital data establish that the central object's behavior is consistent with the supermassive black hole picture, not that the picture has been established as the only possibility.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice C (Surface match): This is the load-bearing trap. It is consistent with the passage — the orbital fit is very constraining, and a black hole is the standard interpretation — but it is not licensed by the stated premises. Showing that one model (a single several-million-solar-mass compact object) reproduces the observations is sufficient evidence for that model's plausibility, but the premises do not establish that every other compact configuration has been formally ruled out. The passage offers a successful fit, not a comprehensive elimination of alternatives, so 'definitively shown to be incompatible with every compact configuration other than a supermassive black hole' overreaches what the orbital data, on their own, can demonstrate.\n- Choice D (Scope shift): Pushes a claim about S0-2's orbit into a sweeping statement about 'every star situated within the central parsec.' The premises describe a single tracked star whose path was followed through a complete loop; they say nothing about whether every other star in that volume is governed by the same point mass.\n- Choice A (Inverse): Directly contradicts premise (2). The passage states that the orbit can be reproduced by a model in which a single compact mass of several million solar masses governs the motion, so the data make a single dominant mass at the center more likely, not less."
