          "band": 4,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "In The Hemingses of Monticello: An American Family (2008), historian Annette Gordon-Reed of Harvard University reconstructs the household at Thomas Jefferson's Monticello by drawing sustained attention to two bodies of material that earlier accounts of the plantation had largely set aside: the testimony preserved by enslaved descendants of the Hemings family and a range of legal records that previous historians had treated as peripheral. Read carefully and at length, those sources document the Hemingses' kinship ties, labor, and movements in detail comparable to that available for the Jeffersons themselves. Because the Hemings family appears as central to the documented record only when these particular sources are taken seriously, Gordon-Reed's reconstruction supports the inference that earlier portrayals of the Hemingses as marginal to Monticello's history primarily reflected ______",
