          "correctAnswer": "D",
          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises: (1) Gordon-Reed reconstructs Monticello by drawing sustained attention to enslaved descendants' testimony and overlooked legal records that earlier historians had set aside, and (2) when those sources are read carefully and at length, they document the Hemingses' kinship, labor, and movements in detail comparable to what is available for the Jeffersons. Because the family becomes central to the documented record only once these particular sources are taken seriously, the minimum inference the premises force is that earlier accounts treated the Hemingses as marginal because of which sources they consulted, not because the surviving evidence itself was inadequate. Choice D states exactly that hedged claim — the source base of the earlier portrayals, 'rather than an inherent feature of the surviving documentary record' — without overreaching what the premises supply.\n\nAt this difficulty, the test is sufficient-vs-necessary: the premises show that engaging the previously sidelined sources is sufficient to make the Hemingses appear central; they do not license stronger claims about what every account must do or about how far the methodological lesson generalizes.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (Surface match): This is the load-bearing trap. It is consistent with the passage's positive framing of Gordon-Reed's reconstruction — once you have read the sources carefully, the centrality of the Hemingses is hard to ignore — but it is not licensed by the premises. The passage establishes that engaging those sources produces a richer documented record; it does not establish that any account omitting the Hemings family thereby fails as documented history. The premises support a claim about why the marginal portrayals looked the way they did (necessity of source choice), not the stronger normative claim that omission is methodologically disqualifying (sufficiency).\n- Choice B (Scope shift): Pushes a claim about earlier portrayals of one plantation household into a sweeping requirement that 'every existing historical narrative of slavery in the early United States' be substantially rewritten. The premises describe a specific reconstruction at Monticello using particular sources; they say nothing about how widely the lesson must propagate or about what every other narrative of early American slavery requires.\n- Choice C (Inverse): Directly contradicts premise (2). The passage states that the testimony of enslaved descendants and the relevant legal records, read carefully and at length, document the Hemingses in detail comparable to the Jeffersons, so those sources are not characterized as too limited to support comparable detail."
