          "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The underlined claim has two specific parts: (1) the apparent silence of Black figures in major American novels is not really an absence, and (2) an Africanist presence structures the canon throughout, organizing the central concerns of the white literary imagination. Choice A maps onto both parts directly. It opens by reframing what \"looks at first like the mere absence of Black figures\" — the silence side of the claim — and then identifies that very silence as \"the very scaffolding on which the work is raised,\" a structuring force. It names the Africanist presence as what defines the white imagination's \"freedom, its terror, and its moral weight,\" matching the claim that this presence shapes the canon's central concerns. That is a direct, scoped match.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice C: surface match — the quotation uses the same key vocabulary (\"Africanist presence,\" \"American writers,\" \"absence\") and sounds like a recognizable Morrison sentence. But its actual subject is the work of Black American writers and how they have had to write within a tradition that ignored them. The claim in the passage is about what shapes the canonical (predominantly white-authored) novel from the inside; Choice C is about the position of Black writers responding to that canon. Same nouns, different argument.\n- Choice B: scope shift — this is on-topic in that it concerns the white American imagination and an Africanist undercurrent, but its scope is wrong. The claim is a general statement about the canon as a whole; Choice B reads one chapter of one Poe novel as illustrative of \"the whole\" imagination. A close reading of a single text is consistent with the claim but does not, on its own, illustrate the canon-wide structuring force the underlined sentence asserts.\n- Choice D: inverse — this quotation actually weakens the claim. It treats the absence of Black characters as \"just that — an absence\" and locates the central concerns of the white American novel in regions \"where race was not, and need not have been, in play.\" That is the precise position Morrison rejects: that the silence is real silence and that race is incidental rather than structuring. Choosing D would have Morrison endorsing the misreading the underlined sentence opposes."
