          "band": 3,
          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
          "passage": "In her 1903 work Optimism: An Essay, Helen Keller distinguishes the cheerful temper that is given to a person at birth from what she calls an inward optimism — a settled outlook on life that the individual must form for herself. Keller, who lost both her sight and her hearing in early childhood, treats optimism not as a fortunate inheritance but as something that has to be earned by sustained labor. *On her account, genuine optimism is not an inherited disposition but a stance the self builds up through repeated effort to meet, rather than evade, the limits that experience sets against it.*",
          "question": "Which quotation from Keller's Optimism: An Essay most effectively illustrates the claim?",
