          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The underlined claim has two specific parts: that genuine optimism, on Keller's account, is (1) not an inherited disposition but (2) a stance the self builds up through repeated effort to meet, rather than evade, the limits experience sets. Choice A maps onto both parts directly. It denies that optimism is \"a gift one is born with\" (the inheritance side of the claim), insists that it is instead \"a habit of mind painfully fashioned\" (the self-formation side), and adds that the soul earns this habit by setting itself \"again and again, to meet its own difficulties rather than to wish them away\" (the repeated-effort-against-limits side). That is a direct, scoped match.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: surface match — the quotation uses Keller's vocabulary of optimism, temper, and weathering trouble, and it sounds like a recognizable early-twentieth-century sentence on hope. But its actual content locates the cheerful temper in what \"Nature, at the very hour of their birth, has given,\" which is precisely the inherited disposition the claim distinguishes from the optimism it describes. The choice is on-topic but does not support the formed-by-effort stance the claim asserts.\n- Choice D: scope shift — this is on-topic in that it concerns Keller's own experience of cheer in the face of difficulty, but its scope is wrong. The claim is a general statement about how genuine optimism is built up over time through repeated effort; Choice D narrates a single afternoon in the garden when one hour of warmth and scent lifted a particular burden. A specific moment of relief does not establish the general, formative discipline the claim describes.\n- Choice C: inverse — this quotation actually weakens the claim. It treats hope as \"the dowry of the human spirit, set within us before we are old enough to ask for it\" and states that even the wisest teacher only uncovers \"what was already lying, fully formed, in the child's heart.\" Choosing D would have Keller endorsing exactly the inheritance view — optimism as a native gift bestowed at birth — that the underlined sentence says she rejects."
