          "correctAnswer": "D",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is D, \", who teaches classics at the University of Cambridge,\". The blank holds a relative clause that adds biographical information about a uniquely identified person, Mary Beard. Because the proper name already pins down who is meant, the clause beginning with \"who\" is nonrestrictive: it does not narrow down which Mary Beard the writer means—it simply adds a fact about her. Standard English requires a nonrestrictive clause to be set off from the rest of the sentence by a matched pair of bracketing marks, most commonly a pair of commas. Choice D supplies exactly that pair, opening with a comma after \"Beard\" and closing with a comma before \"argues,\" which lets the main clause (\"Classical historian Mary Beard … argues in SPQR …\") read cleanly across the inserted information.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice C (\", who teaches classics at the University of Cambridge\"): surface match — this option resembles the correct paired-comma pattern but supplies only the opening comma and never closes the pair. Bracketing punctuation must come in matched pairs (comma–comma, dash–dash, paren–paren); a single opening comma leaves the parenthetical hanging and runs the relative clause directly into the verb \"argues\" with no boundary mark.\n- Choice A (\" who teaches classics at the University of Cambridge,\"): scope shift — a different SEC error. With no comma before \"who,\" the clause is treated as restrictive, as if the writer were picking out one Mary Beard from among several. The trailing comma before \"argues\" then separates the subject (\"Mary Beard who teaches classics at the University of Cambridge\") from its verb (\"argues\"), which Standard English does not permit. Two errors at once, neither of them the correct treatment.\n- Choice B (\" who teaches classics at the University of Cambridge\"): inverse — the opposite of the correct treatment. With no commas at all, the relative clause is read as restrictive and essential to identifying the subject. Because \"Mary Beard\" is already a uniquely identifying proper name, the clause cannot be restrictive; the information it adds is supplementary and must be set off by paired commas, not absorbed into the noun phrase."
