          "explanation": "The pronoun in the blank refers back to the noun phrase 'these novels,' which is plural. Standard English requires a possessive pronoun to agree in number with its antecedent, so the plural antecedent 'these novels' takes the plural possessive 'their.' The resulting phrase, 'their very form . . . quietly underwrites colonial rule,' identifies the form of the novels as the locus of empire's assumptions and preserves number agreement across the long appositive that follows the blank.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (D) 'its' is the surface-match distractor: it is a possessive pronoun that looks like a correct match because the singular noun 'form' appears immediately after the blank, inviting the classic agreement-with-the-nearest-noun error. But the antecedent of the pronoun is 'these novels,' not 'form,' so the singular possessive does not agree.\n- (A) 'his' is the scope-shift distractor: it commits a different SEC error by reaching back past the actual antecedent and treating 'Edward Said' as the referent, producing 'Said's very form,' which is both nonsensical in context and the wrong kind of antecedent (a person rather than the noun phrase 'these novels').\n- (C) 'his or her' is the inverse distractor: it is the singular possessive form used when an antecedent is a generic person of unspecified gender, so it has the opposite number from what the plural antecedent 'these novels' requires."
