          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The blank opens a sentence-initial participial phrase whose implicit subject must match the subject of the main clause. The main-clause subject is 'TallBear,' and the agent who 'maintains that tribal belonging is constituted by political and kinship relations' is TallBear herself. Standard English requires the present active participle, 'Maintaining,' which takes TallBear as its implicit subject and produces a logically attached modifier: 'Maintaining that X, TallBear argues Y.'\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (B) 'Maintained' is the surface-match distractor: a sentence-initial past-participial phrase is grammatical when the subject is the patient of the participle ('Considered the leading expert, TallBear argues...'), but 'maintain that X' has TallBear as the maintainer, not the maintained. Reading 'Maintained that tribal belonging is constituted...' forces a passive interpretation in which TallBear is the thing maintained, producing a dangling modifier.\n- (C) 'Having been maintained' is the inverse distractor: it flips voice and aspect from active simple to perfect passive, again making TallBear the patient rather than the agent of the claim. The implicit subject of the participial phrase still cannot be reconciled with the main-clause subject.\n- (D) 'Maintains' is the scope-shift distractor: it abandons the participial form entirely and substitutes a finite present-tense verb. The result is a subjectless predicate joined to a complete independent clause by a comma, yielding a sentence-fragment-plus-comma-splice structure that tests a different SEC error (sentence boundaries) rather than modifier placement."
