          "id": 751,
          "type": "multiple-choice",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "band": 4,
          "domain": "expression-of-ideas",
          "skill": "transitions",
          "passage": "By the early 1940s, mainstream pediatrics held that infants whose lips and fingertips turned a dusky blue — the children we now call 'blue babies' — were beyond the reach of surgery, and physicians could offer their families little more than reassurance. ______ Helen Brooke Taussig had quietly concluded, after more than a decade of bedside observation and radiographic study at Johns Hopkins, that each distinct pattern of cyanosis traced to a specific structural defect, and that several of those defects could in principle be relieved by an operation on the great vessels just outside the heart.",
          "question": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "id": "A",
              "text": "Nevertheless,"
            },
            {
              "id": "B",
              "text": "Likewise,"
            },
            {
              "id": "C",
              "text": "Instead,"
            },
            {
              "id": "D",
              "text": "By contrast,"
            }
          ],
          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The first sentence establishes a settled consensus held by an entire field: mainstream pediatrics regarded blue babies as untreatable, and physicians offered families nothing beyond reassurance. The second sentence introduces Taussig as a single researcher who, working from more than a decade of her own bedside and radiographic evidence at Johns Hopkins, had reached the opposite conclusion — that the cyanosis tracked specific structural defects and that several of those defects were in principle surgically correctable. The relation is asymmetric concession: despite what the field as a whole believed, this one researcher had quietly concluded otherwise on independent grounds. 'Nevertheless,' is the CB-lexicon transition for that 'despite the prevailing view, Y nonetheless held' move, and is correct (A).\n\nThe single-word cue that selects 'Nevertheless,' here is 'quietly': it frames Taussig's conclusion as held against the grain of an established consensus rather than as one of two openly contending positions. That is the textbook signature of concession rather than parallel contrast.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (D) 'By contrast,' (surface match — different but plausible logical relation) also signals contrast and is the strongest distractor. But 'By contrast,' is calibrated for symmetric A-vs-B framings in which two named, parallel positions are set side by side ('Method A is X. By contrast, Method B is Y.'). Sentence 1 here describes a profession-wide default and sentence 2 describes a lone investigator's contrary finding reached on her own evidence — an asymmetric concession, not a balanced comparison of two parallel views. 'Nevertheless,' captures that asymmetry; 'By contrast,' flattens it.\n- (C) 'Instead,' (scope shift — too strong for the relation) signals outright replacement: in place of doing X, the actor did Y. The two clauses here have different subjects (mainstream pediatrics in the first, Taussig in the second), so Taussig's conclusion is not slotting in as a substitute for a behavior the same actor would otherwise have performed. 'Instead,' overshoots the logical move; the relation is overriding an external consensus, not swapping one of one's own actions for another.\n- (B) 'Likewise,' (inverse — opposite logical move) signals continuation or parallel agreement: it would tell the reader that Taussig's conclusion was on the same line of thought as mainstream pediatrics. Sentence 2 says the opposite — that her conclusion broke from the consensus — so 'Likewise,' points in exactly the wrong direction."
