          "correctAnswer": "B",
          "explanation": "The first two sentences establish a setup. Sentence 1 names a gap in earlier histories: women on both sides of lynching were treated as symbolic figures rather than political actors. Sentence 2 describes the methodological move Feimster makes in Southern Horrors: she recovers women's own testimonies, both as targets and as organizers. Sentence 3 then reports what that recovery produces — an explanation of why anti-lynching activism endured as a sustained Black women's movement. The relation between sentences 2 and 3 is causal-consequential: because Feimster recovered the testimonies, she could explain the puzzle. The CB-lexicon transition for that 'and so this followed' move is 'As a result,' (B).\n\nThe single-word cue that selects 'As a result,' is 'could' in 'she could explain': sentence 3 reports a new explanatory capacity that the archival recovery in sentence 2 enables, framing sentence 3 as the downstream payoff rather than a parallel or contrasting point.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (C) 'For instance,' (surface match — different but plausible logical relation) signals that sentence 3 illustrates the general claim of sentence 2 with a specific case. The reading is plausible because sentence 3 does name a specific outcome. But sentence 3 is not an instance of recovering testimonies; it is what the recovery makes possible — an explanatory consequence — so 'For instance,' mistakes a downstream payoff for an example of the method itself.\n- (A) 'Nevertheless,' (inverse — opposite logical move) signals that sentence 3 holds despite something in sentence 2 — that the explanation arrives in the face of a complication. Sentence 2 raises no complication for sentence 3 to overcome; it provides the very basis for the explanation. 'Nevertheless,' would point in the wrong direction.\n- (D) 'Moreover,' (scope shift — too weak for the relation) treats sentence 3 as one more point added on the same line as sentence 2. That is too flat: sentence 3 is not an additional finding alongside the recovery of testimonies; it is what the recovery yields. 'Moreover,' would fit if the third sentence introduced a separate, parallel contribution of the book; instead, the third sentence reports the explanatory result that the second sentence's archival work makes available."
